The Obstacle Problem: We consider the well-known obstacle problem , . To this end, let \Omega \subseteq \mathbb {R}^d be a bounded domain, and let X:=Y:=H_0^1(\Omega ), Z:=L^2(\Omega ). The obstacle problem considers the minimization problem\min \ f(u) \quad \text{s.t.}\quad u\ge \psi ,where f(u):=\Vert \nabla u\Vert _{L^2(\Omega )}^2 and \psi \in X is a fixed obstacle. In order to formally describe this problem within our framework (REF ), we make the obvious definitiong:X\rightarrow Y, \quad g(u):=\psi -u.Using the Poincaré inequality, it is easy to see that f is strongly convex on X . Hence, the obstacle problem satisfies the requirements of Theorem REF , which implies that the augmented Lagrangian method is globally convergent. Furthermore, since X=Y, it follows that g^{\prime }(u)=-\operatorname{id}_X for every u\in X. Hence, the Zowe-Kurcyusz condition (cf. Definition REF ) is trivially satisfied in every feasible point, which implies the boundedness of the dual iterates (\lambda ^k) by Theorem REF .In fact, the constraint function g satisfies much more than the Zowe-Kurcyusz condition. For every u\in X, the mapping g^{\prime }(u)=-\operatorname{id}_X is bijective. Hence, if a subsequence (x^k)_{\mathcal {K}} converges to a KKT point \bar{x} of (REF ) and \bar{\lambda } is the corresponding multiplier, then we obtainf^{\prime }(x^k)-\lambda ^k=f^{\prime }(x^k)+g^{\prime }(x^k)^* \lambda ^k \rightarrow _{\mathcal {K}} 0.In other words, we see that \lambda ^k \rightarrow _{\mathcal {K}} f^{\prime }(\bar{x})=\bar{\lambda }, i.e. (\lambda ^k)_{\mathcal {K}} converges to the (unique) Lagrange multiplier corresponding to \bar{x}.We now present some numerical results for \Omega :=(0,1)^2 and the obstacle\psi (x,y):=\max \left\lbrace 0.1-0.5 \left\Vert 
\begin{pmatrix}
x-0.5 \\ y-0.5
\end{pmatrix}
\right\Vert ,0\right\rbrace ,cf. Figure REF .

