Tomographic imaging model: We consider general tomographic inverse problems, for which the dependence of the data g_{{\textup {tot}}} from the sought object f can be modeled asg_{{\textup {tot}}} = \begin{pmatrix}
g_{1} \\
\vdots \\
g_{{N_{\textup {proj}}}}
\end{pmatrix}
= \begin{pmatrix}
F_1(P_1 (f)) \\
\vdots \\
F_{{N_{\textup {proj}}}}(P_{{N_{\textup {proj}}}} (f))
\end{pmatrix} = F_{{\textup {tot}}} ( P_{{\textup {tot}}}(f) )P_j are parallel-beam- or cone-beam projectors acting on a 3D-object density f \in L^2(\Omega ) (L^2(\Omega ) := \lbrace f: \mathbb {R}^3 \rightarrow \mathbb {R}: \Vert f \Vert _{L^2} < \infty , \; \operatornamewithlimits{supp}( f ) \subset \Omega \rbrace , \Vert h \Vert _{L^2}^2 := \int |h(x)| ^2 \, x̥) with support \operatornamewithlimits{supp}(f) in a bounded open domain \Omega \subset \mathbb {R}^3. The setting is sketched in fig:sketch.
Note that (REF ) constitutes a semi-continuous tomography model in that f and the g_{1}, \ldots , g_{{N_{\textup {proj}}}} are modeled as continuous images, but the number of tomographic views {N_{\textup {proj}}}\in \mathbb {N} is finite.

