The SART-Scheme:: Analogously to classical SART, the update-formula (REF ) computes the Kaczmarz-iterate in (REF ) via a highly efficient non-iterative scheme, that requires only a single evaluation of {P}_{j_k} and {P}_{j_k}^\ast each:[L^2-regularized SART]{(1)}
Forward-project the current iterate: {p}_k = {P}_{j_k} ({f}_k )

{(2)}
Compute increment in projection space: \Delta {p}_k = ({g}_{j_k} - {p}_k ) \oslash ( {\tilde{u}}_{j_k} + \alpha )

{(3)}
Back-project and increment: {f}_{k+1} = {f}_k + {P}_{j_k}^\ast (\Delta {p}_k )Notably, the actual data-fitting step {(2)} works exclusively on 2D-projections {g}_{j_k}, {p}_k, {\tilde{u}}_{j_k} \in \mathbb {R}^m, which are typically discretized by m = \mathcal {O}(N^2) pixels and thus low-dimensional compared to the discrete 3D-objects {f}_k \in \mathbb {R}^n with n = \mathcal {O}(N^3) (N sampling-points per spatial dimension). This renders step {(2)} cheap to compute, so that the total computational costs of scheme:SART are essentially that of the evaluations of {P}_{j_k} and {P}_{j_k}^\ast in steps {(1)} and {(3)}. Combined with the fast (semi-)convergence of Kaczmarz-methods in \mathcal {O}(1) cycles (see §REF ), this implies that L^2-SART-schemes allow to compute reconstructions at a favorable computational complexity of \mathcal {O}(1) evaluations of the full projector {P}_{{\textup {tot}}} = ({P}_1,\ldots , {P}_{{N_{\textup {proj}}}}) and {P}^\ast _{{\textup {tot}}}, compare §REF .

