Kaczmarz-type splitting and primal-dual methods: Variational reconstruction methods seek to minimize terms of the form \mathcal {S}_{\textup {tot}}\left( P_{\textup {tot}}(f) \right) + \mathcal {R}(f) with \mathcal {S}_{\textup {tot}}(p_1, \ldots , p_{N_{\textup {proj}}}) = \sum _{j=1}^{N_{\textup {proj}}}\mathcal {S}(p_j), compare §REF . Often, this is achieved by some splitting method, alternating (sub-)gradient-descent- (“forward-”) or proximal (“backward-”) iterations with respect to the data-fidelity \mathcal {S}_{\textup {tot}} and the penalty functional \mathcal {R} (or their duals), see e.g. . It is straightforward to combine such an approach with a Kaczmarz-type strategy that exploits the block-structure of \mathcal {S}_{\textup {tot}} to reduce computational costs of the individual iterations. Examples of (primal) Kaczmarz-type splitting methods are given by iterations (\partial \mathcal {R}: subdifferential)f_{k+\frac{1}{2}} &\in \operatornamewithlimits{argmin}_{f \in L^2(\Omega ) } \mathcal {S}_{j_k}\left( P_{j_k} (f) \right) + \frac{1}{2\tau _k} \Vert f - f_k \Vert _{L^2}^2 \\
f_{k+1} &\in {\left\lbrace \begin{array}{ll}
\operatornamewithlimits{argmin}_{f \in L^2(\Omega ) } \mathcal {R}(f) + \frac{1}{2\sigma _k} \Vert f - f_{k+\frac{1}{2}} \Vert _{L^2}^2 &\text{(backward-backward)} \\
f_{k+\frac{1}{2}} - \sigma _k \partial \mathcal {R}(f_{k+\frac{1}{2}}) &\text{(backward-forward)}
\end{array}\right.}with stepsize-parameters \tau _k, \sigma _k. Algorithms of this kind have been proposed and analyzed in , , .
Importantly, the proximal iteration for the data-fidelity, (REF ), can be computed efficiently via the GenSART-scheme from thm:genL2SART. Similarly, GenSART-formulas may be used to compute proximal steps in block-primal-dual methods as considered in .

