Stable reconstruction with noise: In view of , using the same proof we have the following stability bound for the error:

\left\Vert \hat{\omega }- \omega \right\Vert _1 \le \frac{4 \delta }{1-\left\Vert P_{c} q \right\Vert _{\infty }}.

It remains to bound \left\Vert P_{c} q \right\Vert _{\infty } in our discrete grid, namely the values of q_{i,j} outside the set of particles . In order to do so, we shall use the following estimates from \cite [Lemma~2.5]{candes2014towards}:
\begin{equation} 
|\tilde{q}_k(t)| \le 1 - C_1 f_c^2(t-(x_i + k \tau v_i))^2, \quad \textnormal { when } \quad |t - (x_i+k \tau v_i)| \le C_2/f_c,
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
|\tilde{q}_k(t) | \le 1 - C_1 C_2^2, \quad \text{ when } \min _{(x_i,v_i)\in |t-(x_i+k \tau v_i)|> C_2/f_c,
}
where C_1 = 0.3353 and C_2 = 0.1649.
\end{equation}Take (x#, v#) #T, we want to bound the maximum of q(x#,v#). We deal with two cases: when (x#,v#) is close to some particle, i.e.\ (x#,v#) Bi for some i, or when it is not.
Fix i such that (x_\#,v_\#) \in B_i, then we can write (x_\#,v_\#) = (x_i + n_x \Delta _x,v_i + n_v \Delta _v) with (n_x,n_v) \in {Z}\times {Z}\setminus \lbrace (0,0)\rbrace . From the definition of B_i, we have that we can use estimate () for every k \in \mathcal {K}.

