Body: On the basis of the conservation laws of angular momentum and parity , the minimum angular momentum l_{\text{min}} taken away by the \mathcal {\alpha } particle can be obtained by\ l_{\text{min}}=\left\lbrace \begin{array}{llll}
\end{array}{\Delta }_j,&\text{for even${\Delta }_j$ and ${\pi }_p$= ${\pi }_d$},\\
\right.{\Delta }_j+1,&\text{for even${\Delta }_j$ and ${\pi }_p$$\ne $${\pi }_d$},\\j,for odd{\Delta }_j and {\pi }_p\ne {\pi }_d,j+1,for odd{\Delta }_j and {\pi }_p= {\pi }_d,.


where {\Delta }_j= |j_p-j_d|, j_p, \pi _p, j_d, \pi _d denote the spin and parity values of the parent and daughter nuclei, respectively.The nuclear potential V_N(r) is obtained by\ V_N(r)=4\pi \gamma {b}\bar{R}\phi (\xi )
,where \gamma , the surface energy coefficient, is obtained by the Myers and \acute{\text{S}}wiatecki formula and expressed as\ \gamma ={\gamma }_0(1-k_sI^2)
,where I denote the isospin of the parent nucleus. The surface energy constant {\gamma }_0=0.9517 Mev/{\text{fm}}^2 and surface asymmetry constant k_s=1.7826 . The mean curvature radius \bar{R} can be obtained by\ \bar{R}=\frac{C_1C_2}{C_1+C_2}
,where C_i=R_i[1-{(\frac{b}{R_i})}^2] (i=1,2) with C_1 and C_2 representing the matter radius of daughter nucleus and \mathcal {\alpha } particle, respectively. b is the diffuseness of nuclear surface, which is taken as unity. The universal function \phi (\xi ) is expressed as\ \phi (\xi )=\left\lbrace \begin{array}{ll}
\end{array}-\frac{1}{2}{(\xi -2.54)}^2-0.0852{(\xi -2.54)}^3,&\xi \le 1.2511,\\
\right.-3.437\exp (-\frac{\xi }{0.75}),&\xi \ge 1.2511,.


where \xi =(r-C_1-C_2)/b denotes the minimum separation distance.

